      model: "Smartphones and social media have changed the way we live, work and communicate. Many people believe that these technologies are damaging our mental health. In my view, this is partly true, but the problem lies more in how we use technology than in the technology itself. On the one hand, there is growing evidence that heavy use of social media can harm young people's self-esteem. Teenagers constantly compare themselves with perfect images of other people's lives, which can lead to anxiety and loneliness. Moreover, endless notifications are thought to shorten our attention span, making it harder to concentrate on reading or studying. Many adults also feel under pressure to answer work messages late at night, so they never really switch off. On the other hand, technology can support mental wellbeing. Online therapy has made help available to people who live far from a specialist, and apps can teach simple breathing and relaxation techniques. Social media also allows people to stay in touch with friends and family abroad. In conclusion, technology is neither good nor bad in itself. If we make a conscious effort to set limits, such as turning off notifications or keeping phones out of the bedroom, we can enjoy its benefits without paying too high a price."
