    text: "For centuries, doctors have known that some patients feel better after taking treatments that contain no active ingredients at all. In the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, sugar pills were openly given to patients whose complaints could not be explained. Today the placebo effect is studied seriously in laboratories around the world, and it is considered one of the most fascinating puzzles in medicine. In a typical clinical trial, participants are divided into two groups. One group is given the real drug, while the control group receives a placebo that looks identical. Neither the patients nor the doctors are told who has received what. If the real drug is not clearly more effective than the placebo, it is not approved. What has surprised researchers is how powerful the placebo response can be. In trials of treatments for pain, depression and irritable bowel syndrome, a significant proportion of patients in the placebo group report real improvements. How can a pill with nothing in it make someone feel better? It is believed that expectations play a central role. When we expect relief, the brain may release its own natural painkillers, known as endorphins. Brain scans have shown measurable changes in patients who have been given a placebo. Context matters too: placebos are reported to work better when they are expensive, when they are given as injections rather than pills, and when the doctor is warm and confident. Perhaps the strangest finding is that placebos can work even when patients know they are taking one. In several small studies, people with chronic back pain or irritable bowel syndrome were openly given honest placebos and told that the pills contained no medicine. Many still reported feeling better. The results need to be replicated in larger trials, but they raise interesting questions about the ritual of treatment itself. There is also a darker side, known as the nocebo effect. Patients who are warned about possible side effects sometimes experience them even when they have only been given a placebo. In one study, people who were told that a drug might cause tiredness were significantly more likely to report feeling tired, whatever they had actually taken. None of this means that illness is all in the mind. Placebos cannot shrink tumours or cure infections. But the research is a reminder that the way treatment is delivered, including a doctor's words and the trust between doctor and patient, can affect how we feel.",
